code normally and then escape. But since I'm generating JSON directly, I can use a raw string in my thinking, but output must be JSON. I'll construct the JSON manually.\n\nImplementation string:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    segments = case['segments']\n    budget = case['budget']\n    # Validate structure\n    if not isinstance(segments, list) or len(segments) == 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"Invalid or empty segments\")\n    n = len(segments)\n    names = []\n    minima = []\n    capacities = []\n    importances = []\n    for s in segments:\n        names.append(s['name'])\n        minima.append(s['minimum'])\n        capacities.append(s['capacity'])\n        importances.append(s['importance'])\n    sum_min = sum(minima)\n    sum_cap = sum(capacities)\n    if budget < sum_min:\n        raise ValueError(\"Budget below sum of minima\")\n    if budget > sum_cap:\n        raise ValueError(\"Budget exceeds total capacity\")\n    alloc = list(minima)  # copy\n    remaining = budget - sum_min\n    while remaining > 0:\n        best_idx = -1\n        best_imp = 0\n        best_next = 1  # dummy\n        for i in range(n):\n            if alloc[i] >= capacities[i]:\n                continue\n            # next position = alloc[i] + 1\n            if best_idx == -1:\n                best_idx = i\n                best_imp = importances[i]\n                best_next = alloc[i] + 1\n            else:\n                # Compare importance_i / (alloc_i+1) > best_imp / best_next\n                # Cross multiply: importance_i * best_next > best_imp * (alloc_i+1)\n                left = importances[i] * best_next\n                right = best_imp * (alloc[i] + 1)\n                if left > right:\n                    best_idx = i\n                    best_imp = importances[i]\n                    best_next = alloc[i] + 1\n                # else tie: keep earlier (do nothing)\n        if best_idx == -1:\n            # Should not happen because budget <= sum_cap\n            raise ValueError(\"No available segment\")\n        alloc[best_idx] += 1\n        remaining -= 1\n    allocations = {names[i]: alloc[i] for i in range(n)}\n    name_mapping = names  # list in original order\n    total = sum(alloc)\n    return {\"allocations\": allocations, \"name_mapping\": name_mapping, \"total\": total}\n\nWe need to ensure proper indentation. Use 4 spaces.\n\nNow create the JSON. The entire response must be a JSON object with key \"implementation\", value is the string of the Python code. Escape newlines as \\n, backslashes as \\\\, quotes as \\\". Since the code contains single quotes and double quotes, we need to escape double quotes inside the string. I'll use double quotes for the JSON string, so inside the Python code I need to escape any double quotes. The code uses single quotes for dict keys and string literals, so no double quotes. That's convenient. The only double quotes might be in the docstring? None. So no need to escape double quotes. But there are backslashes? No. So just escape newlines.\n\nI'll produce the JSON by constructing the string literal with escaped newlines.\n\nImplementation string (as a single line with \\n):\n\n\"def solve(case):\\n    segments = case['segments']\\n    budget = case['budget']\\n    if not isinstance(segments, list) or len(segments) == 0:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Invalid or empty segments\\\")\\n    n = len(segments)\\n    names = []\\n    minima = []\\n    capacities = []\\n    importances = []\\n    for s in segments:\\n        names.append(s['name'])\\n        minima.append(s['minimum'])\\n        capacities.append(s['capacity'])\\n        importances.append(s['importance'])\\n    sum_min = sum(minima)\\n    sum_cap = sum(capacities)\\n    if budget < sum_min:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Budget below sum of minima\\\")\\n    if budget > sum_cap:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Budget exceeds total capacity\\\")\\n    alloc = list(minima)\\n    remaining = budget - sum_min\\n    while remaining > 0:\\